# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config, so on a Windows
# machine the default is to rewrite line endings on checkout. For most of a repo
# that is harmless. For the files below it is not:
#
#   * a shell script checked out with CRLF fails at the shebang — `sh` reads the
#     trailing \r as part of the interpreter path, and the error names a file
#     that plainly exists.
#   * a Makefile is whitespace-significant; the same scripts are also COPYed
#     into Linux images, where \r never becomes acceptable.
#
# Pinning eol=lf here means these files are byte-identical on every platform no
# matter what a given developer's core.autocrlf happens to be — the repo stops
# depending on per-machine Git configuration. See docs/26-windows.md.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# Windows-only helpers are the exception: cmd.exe requires CRLF in batch files.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Vendored golden references are hashed BYTE-FOR-BYTE in their PROVENANCE.md,
# which is the tamper check third_party/README.md requires. Line-ending
# normalisation would rewrite them on checkout — on Windows, where
# core.autocrlf=true is the Git-for-Windows default, `basic.json` gains 320
# bytes and its sha256 no longer matches the one recorded on the machine that
# vendored it. `-text` disables the conversion in both directions, so a
# vendored artifact is identical to upstream on every platform.
#
# Applies to the whole tree rather than to `*.json`: whatever a future
# reference happens to be (a licence, a .proto, a .csv), it is still hashed.
third_party/** -text
